# Store screenshots

Everything about how {{appName}}'s store screenshots look lives here, in git,
reviewed like code. Nobody opens a design tool to change a headline.

## The pieces

| File | What it controls |
|---|---|
| `Framefile.json` | bezel, background, padding, title colour and font |
| `<locale>/title.strings` | the headline under each screenshot, per locale |
| `background.png` | optional. Commit one to override the flat colour the workflow paints |

The screenshots themselves come from `integration_test/screenshot_test.dart`.
Add a destination there and it appears in the listing; the numbered capture
name is what ties the three together.

## Changing a headline

Edit `<locale>/title.strings`. Keys are the capture filenames:

```
"01_home" = "Everything in one place";
```

**Keep the numeric prefix.** frameit matches a key as a case-insensitive
substring of the screenshot's *full path*, so a bare `"home"` would also match
every file under `/home/runner/...` on a Linux runner and put that headline on
all of them.

These are store copy, not UI strings: write the benefit, not the feature name.
The `app-store-optimization` skill covers what belongs here — and note that
most people never scroll past the first two.

## Regenerating

From the repo, run the **Screenshots (Android)**{{#ios}} or **Screenshots
(iOS)**{{/ios}} workflow from the Actions tab. Both are manual, take a locale,
and commit the framed result to the branch you ran them on.
{{#ios}}
Android runs on Ubuntu; only iOS needs a macOS runner.
{{/ios}}

Locally:

```sh
flutter drive \
  --driver=test_driver/integration_test.dart \
  --target=integration_test/screenshot_test.dart \
  --dart-define=SCREENSHOT_LOCALE={{#hasNepali}}ne{{/hasNepali}}{{^hasNepali}}en{{/hasNepali}} \
  -d <device>

cd android && bundle exec fastlane screenshots locale:en-US
```

Framing needs ImageMagick (`brew install imagemagick`, or
`apt-get install imagemagick`).

## Where the output goes

- Play — `android/fastlane/metadata/android/<locale>/images/phoneScreenshots/`
{{#ios}}
- App Store — `ios/fastlane/screenshots/<locale>/`
{{/ios}}

Both are the directories `supply`{{#ios}} and `deliver`{{/ios}} upload from, so
a committed screenshot is already in the release path — no separate step.

## Device size is not free choice

frameit picks a device frame by **exact resolution** and fails on a size it
does not recognise. That constrains which emulator and simulator you capture
on, so the workflow defaults are not arbitrary:

| Store | Capture on | Resolution |
|---|---|---|
| Play | `pixel_5` emulator | 1080 x 2340 |
{{#ios}}
| App Store | iPhone 16 Pro Max simulator | 1320 x 2868 |
{{/ios}}

Pixel 5 is the newest Android frame fastlane ships — `pixel_6` is 1080 x 2400
and has no frame. {{#ios}}On the Apple side 1320 x 2868 is both a frameit size
and the 6.9" screenshot Apple requires, which is a happy coincidence rather
than a rule; iPhone 17 Pro Max is the same size. {{/ios}}If you change the
profile, check the size against fastlane's `device_types.rb` first. The
workflow prints every capture's dimensions before framing so a mismatch is
obvious.

## A caveat worth knowing

frameit's device frames are a fixed, ageing set; they will not always be this
year's phone. For the App Store specifically, [goldie](https://github.com/kacperkapusciak/goldie)
has current frames, nicer layouts and can build the preview video — but it
needs macOS and does not do Android, so it is a local Mac tool rather than
something these workflows can run for both stores.
